_gate_rev_durability":0.13468963015226554,"_gp_rev_durability":true,"_score_rev_durability":92.3,"_gate_fcf_durability":null,"_gp_fcf_durability":null,"_score_fcf_durability":null,"_gate_margins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":null,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":null,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.014369798370507204,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":27.1,"_gate_share_shrink":0.04381042440203231,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":null,"_gp_sbc_dilution":null,"_score_sbc_dilution":0.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.12665,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":84.4,"_gate_insider_buying":0.0,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Southern Missouri Bancorp, Inc. operates as the bank holding company for Southern Bank that provides banking and financial services to individuals and corporate customers in the United States. The company offers deposit products, including interest-bearing and noninterest-bearing transaction accounts, money market deposit accounts, savings accounts, certificates of deposit, and retirement savings plans. It also provides loans secured by mortgages on one- to four-family residences and commercial and agricultural real estate; construction loans on residential and commercial properties; commercial and agricultural business loans; consumer loans; and business financing, including small business, working capital, business real estate, and equipment loans. In addition, the company offers fiduciary and investment management services; brokerage services of commercial and consumer insurance products; cash management and merchant services; online and mobile banking services; refinance services; and debit or credit cards. The company was founded in 1887 and is headquartered in Poplar Bluff, Missouri.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Greg A. Steffens | Chairman & CEO | Age 58 | Compensation: $680,457 (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 711 people. Revenue per employee of $282K ranks in the bottom quartile among Financial Services peers, improving at 14%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $108K of free cash flow annually. CEO compensation is 2\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (5/10).","financial_summary":["Southern Missouri Bancorp, Inc. is a small-cap financial services business with a $775M market cap on $201M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +9% over the past three years and +13% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size. Operating margins are expanding by +2.5pp year-over-year, which is one of the strongest signals that the moat is deepening.","Returns on capital run at 15% against a 8% cost of capital, a 7-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it.","The business throws off $77M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 38 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.12\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and interest coverage runs at 11.1\u00d7.","Shareholder yield is only 1.4%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 13% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and within its sector the business captures 1.7\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.2) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Insiders are overwhelmingly selling (100% sell ratio) \u2014 the people who know the business want less of it","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","ratio":0.0},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 14% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Trading at a 68% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","30 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Southern Missouri Bancorp (NASDAQ:SMBC) Releases Quarterly Earnings Results, Beats Expectations By $0.07 EPS","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/10/21/southern-missouri-bancorp-nasdaqsmbc-releases-quarterly-earnings-results-beats-expectations-by-0-07-eps.html","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":1794002165.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.361,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"Contrasting Southern Missouri Bancorp (NASDAQ:SMBC) & Banco Santander (NYSE:SAN)","source":"th